---
// Lesson 2.3a — Health Potions & the Max-Health Cap
// 3-way comparative analysis: Coin vs Spike vs Potion.
// Multiple functions for bounding values (if vs Mathf.Min vs Mathf.Clamp).
// Creating the Potion prefab and implementing selection algorithms in StatManager.cs.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.3a — Health Potions &amp; the Max-Health Cap</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You can collect wealth (coins) and suffer damage (spikes). Now you need a way to restore life.
    Today, you compare the potion to the coin and the spike, discover why healing without a limit
    creates a game-breaking exploit, and evaluate three different Unity functions for enforcing
    boundary rules using <strong>selection</strong>.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Comparative Thinking</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Notice how our game systems build on each other. When designing the Potion,
        we ask: <em>what have we already built that works like this?</em>
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Analysis</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion similar to the coin?</strong></td>
            <td>Both are <strong>pickups</strong>: intangible trigger colliders (<code>Is Trigger = true</code>), handled by <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code>, and destroyed immediately upon collection so they cannot be grabbed twice.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion similar to the spike?</strong></td>
            <td>Both affect <strong>health</strong> on the <code>StatManager</code>, unlike coins which affect wealth.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>How is the potion different from both?</strong></td>
            <td>It heals (+1 health), but introduces a brand-new constraint: <strong>health must never exceed maximum capacity</strong> (e.g. 3 hearts). Without a rule, drinking 10 potions gives 13 health!</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Enforcing this rule is what the AP CSP framework calls <strong>selection</strong>:
        using conditional logic to decide whether or how state changes.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Multiple Ways to Cap a Value</h3>
        <p>
            In Unity C#, there are three common ways to ensure a value does not exceed a maximum limit:
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Code Example</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>1. Procedural <code>if</code></strong></td><td><code>if (health &gt; maxHealth) health = maxHealth;</code></td><td>Direct selection statement. Simple, easy to read for humans.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>2. <code>Mathf.Min</code></strong></td><td><code>health = Mathf.Min(health + amount, maxHealth);</code></td><td>Mathematical function. Returns the smaller of two numbers.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>3. <code>Mathf.Clamp</code></strong></td><td><code>health = Mathf.Clamp(health + amount, 0, maxHealth);</code></td><td>Boundary clamp. Restricts a value between a minimum (0) and maximum.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is <code>Mathf.Clamp</code> particularly powerful when managing health? How could a single clamp function handle both dying (falling below 0) and overhealing (rising above maxHealth)?</li>
            <li>In what scenarios is an explicit <code>if</code> statement better than <code>Mathf.Min</code> or <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>? (Hint: What if you want to play a special "Buzz / Error" sound only when the player tries to drink at full health?)</li>
            <li>Where should this capping logic live — in the Potion script or in <code>StatManager.cs</code>? Why is placing the rule in <code>StatManager</code> better software design?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — Create the Potion Prefab.</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>In the Hierarchy, right-click → <strong>2D Object → Sprite</strong>, name it <strong>Potion</strong>.</li>
        <li>Assign a potion sprite from <code>Assets/Artwork</code>.</li>
        <li>Add a <strong>BoxCollider2D</strong> or <strong>CircleCollider2D</strong>, and check <strong>Is Trigger</strong>.</li>
        <li>At the top of the Inspector, click Tag → <strong>Add Tag...</strong>, create the tag <strong>Potion</strong>, and assign it to the Potion.</li>
        <li>Drag the Potion GameObject into <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> to make it a reusable prefab!</li>
    </ol>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Notice how different mathematical capping techniques operate in isolation across completely different systems:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Selection Guard on a Water Reservoir Dam:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Uses an if statement to enforce an upper ceiling
if (currentGallons &gt; maxReservoirCapacity)
{
    currentGallons = maxReservoirCapacity;
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Mathematical Ceiling with Mathf.Min on an Electric Scooter Battery:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Mathf.Min returns the smaller value, preventing the battery from charging past 100%
batteryLevel = Mathf.Min(batteryLevel + chargeAdded, 100f);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Two-Sided Bounding with Mathf.Clamp on a Spaceship Thruster:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Mathf.Clamp guarantees the output stays between minimum idle (0) and maximum safe power (100)
thrusterPower = Mathf.Clamp(requestedThrust, 0, 100);</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If <code>currentGallons</code> is <code>95</code> and <code>20</code> gallons are added, trace Example 1: what is the value before and after the <code>if</code> check?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between <code>Mathf.Min</code> (one upper bound) and <code>Mathf.Clamp</code> (both lower and upper bounds)?</li>
            <li>Where should health-capping logic live — on the potion GameObject, or inside <code>StatManager</code>? Why is placing the rule in the central manager better software design?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — Create the Potion Prefab.</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>In the Hierarchy, right-click → <strong>2D Object → Sprite</strong>, name it <strong>Potion</strong>.</li>
        <li>Assign a potion sprite from <code>Assets/Artwork</code>.</li>
        <li>Add a <strong>BoxCollider2D</strong> or <strong>CircleCollider2D</strong>, and check <strong>Is Trigger</strong>.</li>
        <li>At the top of the Inspector, click Tag → <strong>Add Tag...</strong>, create the tag <strong>Potion</strong>, and assign it to the Potion.</li>
        <li>Drag the Potion GameObject into <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> to make it a reusable prefab!</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>StatManager.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Max Health Knob:</strong> Declare <code>[SerializeField] private int maxHealth = 3;</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Health Ceiling Guard:</strong> Inside <code>ChangeHealth(int amount)</code>, after modifying <code>health</code>, implement a ceiling check (using an <code>if</code> conditional, <code>Mathf.Min</code>, or <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>) to guarantee that <code>health</code> can never exceed <code>maxHealth</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>PlayerController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Handle Potion Trigger:</strong> Inside your existing <code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code> method, add an additional branch to check <code>other.CompareTag("Potion")</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Heal &amp; Destroy:</strong> If matched, check <code>StatManager.manager != null</code>, call <code>StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(1);</code>, and destroy the collected potion object (<code>Destroy(other.gameObject);</code>).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save both files and press Play!
        Walk into spikes to drop your health to 2, then walk into a potion: your health restores to 3.
        Walk into another potion while at full health: your health stays capped at 3 instead of climbing to 4!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Testing Boundary Conditions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you change <code>maxHealth</code> to <code>5</code> in the Inspector? Does the code still cap correctly, or did you hardcode the number 3 anywhere?</li>
            <li>What happens if you accidentally write <code>health &lt; maxHealth</code> in the capping check? Trace what happens when health is 2.</li>
            <li>What happens if you forget to check <strong>Is Trigger</strong> on the Potion prefab? Does <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> run?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Testing Mathf.Clamp</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In <code>StatManager.cs</code>, replace the <code>if (health &gt; maxHealth)</code> block with:
            <pre><code>health = Mathf.Clamp(health + amount, 0, maxHealth);</code></pre>
            Test taking damage on spikes and healing with potions. Does this one function replace multiple if statements?</li>
            <li>Game Design Question: Should a player at full health be allowed to consume a potion and waste it, or should the potion stay on the ground until needed? How would you implement that check in <code>PlayerController.cs</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain how potions are similar to coins, similar to spikes, and different from both</li>
            <li>Define selection and explain how it prevents the overheal exploit</li>
            <li>Compare three ways to bound values in C# (<code>if</code>, <code>Mathf.Min</code>, <code>Mathf.Clamp</code>)</li>
            <li>Create a new prefab with appropriate collider settings and custom tags</li>
            <li>Commit your working potion healing system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — StatManager, selection, and pickups</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — reference guide for trigger pickups</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Mathf and selection syntax</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.4a.html">2.4a — Prefabs, Spawning &amp; Playtesting</a></p>
</div>`;
